WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_dkn_141'), 'The compose file has no `volumes:` anywhere. After a config change the team ran:

```text
$ docker compose down
[+] Running 4/4
 Container rice-rocket-api-1      Removed
 Container rice-rocket-migrate-1  Removed
 Container rice-rocket-db-1       Removed
 Network rice-rocket_default      Removed
$ docker compose up -d
$ docker compose exec db psql -U rocket -d rocket -tc ''select count(*) from orders''
     0
$ docker volume ls
DRIVER    VOLUME NAME
local     3f9a0c7be1d24e6a9c1f0b8e5d7a2c4f6e8b0a1d3c5e7f9b2d4f6a8c0e2b4d6f
local     9b1e5d3c7a0f2e4b6d8c0a2e4f6b8d0c2e4a6b8d0f2c4e6a8b0d2f4c6e8a0b2d
```

Statement: "`docker compose down` deleted the database files, and a named volume would not have helped, because `down` removes every volume of the project."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. `docker compose down` removes containers and networks; it removes named volumes only with `-v`. The postgres image declares a VOLUME for /var/lib/postgresql/data, so without a volume in the compose file Docker created an anonymous volume. The old data still sits in one of the two hash-named volumes listed above, but the new db container got a fresh anonymous volume because nothing ties the old one to it. A named volume (`pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data` plus a top-level `volumes: pgdata:`) is reattached by name on the next `up`. The misconception is that `down` is what wipes data; the missing name is.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_dkn_141') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
