WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_dkn_161'), 'Monthly report exports fail; nginx logs this for each of them:

```text
2026/11/02 09:14:03 [error] 29#29: *8812 upstream timed out (110: Connection timed out) while reading response header from upstream, client: 203.0.113.7, server: ricerocket.vn, request: "GET /api/reports/monthly?month=2026-10 HTTP/1.1", upstream: "http://172.18.0.4:3000/reports/monthly?month=2026-10"
203.0.113.7 - - [02/Nov/2026:09:14:03 +0700] "GET /api/reports/monthly?month=2026-10 HTTP/1.1" 504 167 rt=60.001
```

Statement: "Raising `proxy_read_timeout` for the reports location fixes these 504s; `proxy_connect_timeout` is not involved, because the connection was open and nginx was waiting for the response."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. "while reading response header from upstream" after exactly 60 s is proxy_read_timeout (default 60s): nginx connected, sent the request, and the api sent nothing back within 60 s. proxy_read_timeout counts the time between two successive reads, not the whole response, so a report that takes 4 minutes before its first byte needs a larger value in its own location. A connect failure would log "while connecting to upstream" instead. Raising the timeout for every location is the common overreaction: it also lets a stuck request on any other endpoint hold a connection for minutes.', 3, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_dkn_161') AND order_index = 3)
    RETURNING id
)
