```nginx
limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=login:10m rate=10r/m;

location = /api/login {
    limit_req zone=login burst=5 nodelay;
    proxy_pass http://api/login;
}
```

One IP then sends 20 login requests within the same second:

```text
198.51.100.23 "POST /api/login" x 20 at 10:02:07
```

Statement: "Because of `nodelay`, all 20 requests reach the api immediately; the limit only slows down requests in the following minute."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. nodelay only means that requests inside the burst are not spaced out: the first request plus the 5 burst slots (6 in total) go to the api at once, and the other 14 are rejected straight away with limit_req_status, 503 by default (set 429). The bucket then refills at the rate, one slot every 6 seconds for 10r/m. A common misconception is that nodelay disables or defers the limit; it changes only the delay of requests that are within the burst.', 3, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_dkn_171') AND order_index = 3)
    RETURNING id
)
